# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: bricscad-undocumented-clisms — inventory of BricsCAD V26 undocumented Common Lisp constructs
#+STATUS: BLOCKED
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: bricsys-support (vendor confirmation of the setf-"2" sentinel)
#+BRANCH: work-unblocked-p1

title: BricsCAD V26: inventory of undocumented Common Lisp constructs
status: open
type: enhancement + spec
filed: 2026-06-07
component: autolisp-spec :: vendor-conformance + clautolisp dialect
        warnings
related: variadic-functions.issue (the `&'-rest case, now
         implemented in clautolisp 1.1.16 as a non-strict
         extension)
         deferred-bricscad-setq-nil-returns-2.issue (the trigger
         that surfaced this whole inventory: the `2' was actually
         from `setf' the user typed instead of `setq', and `setf'
         turned out to be one of the undocumented constructs)

== Summary ==

BricsCAD V26 silently accepts several Common Lisp constructs that
do NOT appear in Autodesk's public AutoLISP reference and do NOT
appear in any of the vendor docs the autolisp-spec project has
catalogued (see `autolisp-spec/documentation/autolisp-reference-notes.org'
and `autolisp-spec/documentation/autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org').

Confirmed by Bricsys support + user repro on 2026-06-07:

  - `&rest' in defun / lambda lambda-lists. The autolisp-spec
    project's `variadic-functions.issue' originally proposed the
    bare `&' separator (no `rest' keyword); BricsCAD V26 uses the
    Common Lisp spelling `&REST' (case-insensitive, like every
    AutoLISP symbol). User confirmed with a session transcript:

        alfe> (defun foo (&rest args)
                (list 'args '= args 'of-type (type args) (type-of args)))
        FOO
        alfe> (foo 1 2 3)
        (ARGS = (1 2 3) OF-TYPE LIST <CONS>)

    The bare `&' (clautolisp's original spelling) is NOT accepted
    by BricsCAD.

    Earlier vendor-bug suspicion FALSE-FLAGGED + RESOLVED:
    `issues/closed/bricscad-rest-mapcar-lambda-bug.issue' was
    initially filed against BricsCAD as a `&rest'/mapcar interaction
    defect. User's clean repro then proved the failing form runs
    cleanly in raw BricsCAD V26; the bug was actually on alfe's
    side, in autolisp-bootstrap.lsp's `autolisp-normalize-princ-call'
    unconditionally re-consing every cell of the user's form. Fixed
    in alfe 1.1.10 by making normalize cons-identity-preserving
    when there's no `(princ)' to rewrite.

Observed by user, not yet probed against a fresh BricsCAD session:

  - `setf' — returns `2' on `(setf *sym* nil)' (this is what
    surfaced the issue; user initially mis-read the displayed `2'
    as a bug in `setq', see the related issue above).
  - `let' — generalised CL let with parallel bindings.
  - `let*' — CL let* with sequential bindings.

The above three need vendor-confirmation probes before we can
treat them as load-bearing extensions.

== Why this matters ==

Users writing AutoLISP that's intended to be portable across
vendors will hit silent BricsCAD-only behavior. Today the spec
draft and the reader/runtime dialect framework have no way to
flag uses of these constructs — they neither warn nor error, they
just work on BricsCAD and break on AutoCAD.

In the other direction, clautolisp aims to be a faithful AutoLISP
host: if BricsCAD accepts a construct, clautolisp should too (at
least under `--dialect bricscad'). The variadic-functions.issue
just landed the first piece (`&'-rest); the other three are
candidates for follow-up.

== Plan ==

1. Probe inventory. Write probes under
   `autolisp-spec/sources/probe-bricscad-extensions.lsp' that run
   in a BricsCAD console and report exactly what each construct
   does:

     (princ "1. setq returns ")  (princ (setq foo 99))   (terpri)
     (princ "2. setf returns ")  (princ (setf foo nil))  (terpri)
     (princ "3. let returns  ")  (princ (let ((x 1) (y 2)) (+ x y))) (terpri)
     (princ "4. let* returns ")  (princ (let* ((x 1) (y (+ x 1))) y)) (terpri)
     (princ "5. &rest binds  ")  (princ ((lambda (a &rest r) r) 1 2 3 4)) (terpri)
     (princ "6. & binds     ")  (princ ((lambda (a & r) r) 1 2 3 4)) (terpri)

   Gather one run's transcript into
   `autolisp-spec/results/bricscad/macos/<TIMESTAMP>-undocumented-clisms/'
   the way other probe runs already organise themselves.

2. Spec page. Add
   `autolisp-spec/documentation/bricscad-undocumented-extensions.org'
   listing each construct with: vendor-confirmation evidence
   (Bricsys ticket id, probe transcript date), observed semantics,
   recommendation (portability hazard | clautolisp-implementable
   | clautolisp-shouldn't-implement).

3. clautolisp implementations.

   a. `&'-rest in defun/lambda. DONE (1.1.16, variadic-functions.issue).
   b. `setf' — value semantics: which places does BricsCAD's
      `setf' actually support, and how does it differ from `setq'
      on symbol places? Probe before implementing. If symbol-only,
      it's essentially an alias for `setq' and can be implemented
      trivially.
   c. `let' / `let*' — these are NICE additions but require
      careful lambda-list / dynamic-frame design. Implement only
      after the probes confirm BricsCAD's exact semantics
      (parallel vs sequential binding, scope of the let body,
      interaction with `/'-locals).

4. Dialect warnings. Under `--dialect bricscad' (the lax-but-vendor-
   faithful target), each of these constructs should still be
   accepted but emit `[ext-bricscad-undocumented] LET/LET*/SETF/&'
   diagnostics so users writing portable code are alerted. Under
   `--dialect autodesk' or `--dialect strict' they should error
   with `:strict-lambda-list-extension' / `:undocumented-construct'
   so portable-code scanners flag them.

== Notes ==

- The autolisp-spec draft (`autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org')
  was searched on 2026-06-07 for `&rest', `setf', `let' (sole-symbol),
  `variadic'. NONE appear. The reference-notes file lists only
  Autodesk URLs; we have no offline copy of the Autodesk PDF to
  search.

- Bricsys support is the ONLY confirming source for `&rest' so
  far. That confirmation is what unblocked the clautolisp
  implementation in variadic-functions.issue. We should re-engage
  Bricsys support with a single ticket listing setf/let/let* and
  ask whether each is officially supported, officially
  deprecated, or officially undocumented-but-stable.

- This issue is intentionally an UMBRELLA: the actual code
  changes will land in per-construct branches/commits; this file
  tracks the meta-decision (which constructs to chase, in what
  order) and the documentation rollout.

== Progress (2026-08-06) — Plan step 1 (probe) authored + CI-wired ==

The harvest probe is written and wired; only the one-click run on real
BricsCAD remains (pjb, on the now-available runners).

  - Probe: autolisp-front-end/tests/scenarios/entities/bricscad-extensions-probe.lsp.
    It captures each construct's RAW RETURN VALUE directly — each result is
    wrapped in its own princ, so what is recorded is the form's own return
    value, not any REPL/print-wrap echo (this is what
    deferred-bricscad-setq-nil-returns-2 needs to rule out the alfe layer).
    setq baseline; setf against nil/1/2/99/"hello"/'(a b) + a
    setf-then-read to tell "returns a sentinel but assigns correctly" from
    "does not assign"; let (parallel-binding discipline), let* (sequential);
    &rest and the bare & separator. Every construct goes through
    vl-catch-all-apply, so an unsupported one records "ERROR:<msg>" instead
    of aborting — the probe runs to completion on ANY backend. Output is
    machine-readable "EXTPROBE <KEY>=<VALUE>", ending "EXTPROBE DONE".

  - CI: NO new job needed. The probe is registered in the existing
    vendor-probes drivers (scripts/run-vendor-probes.{sh,ps1}); a probe with
    no per-vendor .sexp expectation file is now treated as HARVEST — its
    transcript is saved as the artifact and it never gates the pipeline
    (green). So the existing manual jobs vendor:probes:bricscad (Windows) and
    vendor:probes:bricscad:macos already carry it. To harvest: trigger
    either job; read dist/vendor-probes/bricscad/bricscad-extensions.log.

  - Validated locally end-to-end via the clautolisp backend
    (bash scripts/run-vendor-probes.sh --backend clautolisp): the three
    conformance probes still PASS and bricscad-extensions reports HARVEST
    with its transcript. clautolisp's own current behaviour (documents the
    "faithful host" gap):
      SETQ -> value; SETF/LET/LET* -> ERROR:Undefined (not implemented);
      &rest AND bare & both bind (2 3 4).

  - Next after the BricsCAD harvest: Plan steps 2-4 (spec page
    bricscad-undocumented-extensions.org with the transcript evidence;
    per-construct clautolisp impl decisions — setf likely a setq alias on
    symbol places once the return is decoded; let/let* after semantics are
    pinned; the [ext-bricscad-undocumented] dialect warnings).

== Harvest RESULTS (2026-08-07, real BricsCAD V26 / macOS runner) ==

vendor:probes:bricscad:macos (pipeline on master @ b97d9fd) ran the probe
against real BricsCAD. Transcript
(dist/vendor-probes/bricscad/bricscad-extensions.log):

  EXTPROBE SETQ-99=99          EXTPROBE SETQ-NIL=nil
  EXTPROBE SETF-NIL=2          EXTPROBE SETF-1=2      EXTPROBE SETF-2=2
  EXTPROBE SETF-99=2           EXTPROBE SETF-STRING=2 EXTPROBE SETF-LIST=2
  EXTPROBE SETF-THEN-FOO=nil
  EXTPROBE LET-SUM=3           EXTPROBE LET-PARALLEL=(1 10)
  EXTPROBE LETSTAR-SEQ=ERROR:no function definition <LET*>
  EXTPROBE REST-BINDS=(2 3 4)  EXTPROBE AMP-BINDS=ERROR:too few / too many

Confirmed per-construct (this is the vendor-confirmation evidence Plan
step 2/3 wanted):

  - setf : returns the CONSTANT 2 for every value (nil/1/2/99/"hello"/
    '(a b)) => the "2" is an INTERNAL SENTINEL, not the assigned value.
    And SETF-THEN-FOO=nil: after (setf foo 42), foo is still nil => setf
    does NOT assign at all on a symbol place. So BricsCAD's `setf' is NOT
    a setq alias; it is a quirky non-assigning op returning a fixed 2.
    DECISION (revises Plan step 3b): clautolisp must NOT imitate this.
    If clautolisp ever offers `setf' it should be a clean clautolisp
    EXTENSION (CL-style generalised assignment), never the BricsCAD "2"
    behaviour; under --dialect bricscad, `setf' on a symbol is best left
    unbound/emitting [ext-bricscad-undocumented] rather than faked.
    This also resolves deferred-bricscad-setq-nil-returns-2 (closed).

  - let : real PARALLEL binding confirmed — (setq x 10)(let ((x 1)(y x))
    (list x y)) => (1 10) (y sees the OUTER x, not the let's x=1).
    LET-SUM=3. So `let' IS a load-bearing BricsCAD extension worth
    implementing in clautolisp with parallel-binding semantics.

  - let* : DOES NOT EXIST on BricsCAD V26 ("no function definition
    <LET*>"). This REFUTES the issue's original assumption that let* is a
    silent extension. Do NOT add let* under --dialect bricscad; if
    clautolisp wants let* it is a pure clautolisp extension.

  - &rest : works ((2 3 4)); bare `&' : rejected ("too few / too many").
    Confirms the &rest spelling already implemented (variadic-functions),
    and that bare `&' must NOT be accepted under --dialect bricscad.

Remaining (Plan steps 2-4, unchanged): write
bricscad-undocumented-extensions.org from this transcript; implement `let'
(parallel) as the one genuine extension; wire [ext-bricscad-undocumented]
warnings for setf/let*/& under the strict/autocad dialects. A Bricsys
support ticket to confirm the setf-"2" is intentional is still wanted.

== Progress (2026-08-07) — Plan steps 2-4 (spec page, `let', warnings, tests) ==

Landed on branch feature-bricscad-undocumented-clisms (clautolisp 1.8.9).

DONE:

  - Spec page. autolisp-spec/documentation/bricscad-undocumented-extensions.org
    (org-mode, UTF-8, mode/coding header). One section per construct
    (setf / let / let* / &rest / bare-&), each with the 2026-08-07
    vendor:probes:bricscad:macos harvest evidence (the EXTPROBE values),
    observed semantics, and the clautolisp recommendation. Marked
    explicitly as a vendor-extensions catalogue, NOT promoted into the
    normative spec draft.

  - `let' implemented (the one genuine extension). New EVAL-LET-FORM +
    PARSE-LET-BINDING in clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp,
    registered as ("LET" . #'eval-let-form) in *special-operator-dispatch*.
    CL-style PARALLEL binding: every init-form is evaluated in the
    enclosing scope first, then the vars are bound simultaneously in a
    fresh dynamic frame (shadow torn down on normal + non-local exit, like
    foreach / usubr /-locals). Body is an implicit progn (empty => nil).
    Bindings: bare symbol (=> nil) or (symbol init-form). Reproduces the
    harvest exactly: (setq x 10)(let ((x 1)(y x))(list x y)) => (1 10),
    and (let ((x 1)(y 2))(+ x y)) => 3.

  - Dialect warnings. New EMIT-BRICSCAD-UNDOCUMENTED-WARNING reuses the
    existing ch.25 portability-warning infra (dialect-name gate +
    %portability-warning-occurrence-seen-p once-per-occurrence dedup +
    portability-warning-mode :error escalation, to *error-output*). `let'
    emits [ext-bricscad-undocumented] under --dialect strict / autocad;
    silent under bricscad / clautolisp / lax. bare-& / &rest keep firing
    via the pre-existing [lambda-list-extension] emitter.

  - Tests (FiveAM), added to
    clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/tests/evaluator-tests.lisp: LET parallel
    binding (the (1 10) harvest case), LET-SUM=3, implicit-progn body +
    return value, empty body => nil, bare-symbol => nil, shadow
    teardown, LET x /-locals interaction, malformed binding
    (:invalid-let-binding), and the [ext-bricscad-undocumented] gate
    (silent bricscad/clautolisp/lax, warns strict/autocad, once-per-
    occurrence, :error escalation), plus a guard test that `let*' is NOT
    a special operator (stays :undefined-function).

  Verification (SBCL, both suites 0 failures):
    make -C clautolisp test-sbcl TEST_SYSTEM=clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/tests
        => Did 422 checks. Pass: 422 (100%). Fail: 0.
    make -C clautolisp test-sbcl TEST_SYSTEM=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/tests
        => Did 1776 checks. Pass: 1776 (100%). Fail: 0.

DECIDED / NOT done (deliberately):

  - `setf' NOT implemented and NOT imitated. The harvest proved BricsCAD's
    setf returns a constant sentinel 2 and does NOT assign; faking that is
    worthless. clautolisp leaves setf undefined (=> :undefined-function).
  - `let*' NOT added. Refuted by the harvest ("no function definition
    <LET*>"); not a BricsCAD extension. Stays :undefined-function.

TODO (low value, left as a clearly-marked follow-up):

  - Dedicated [ext-bricscad-undocumented] diagnostic for setf / let*.
    Both are intentionally undefined, so today a use raises
    :undefined-function rather than a targeted extension note. Emitting the
    dedicated note would need a small recognise-but-don't-define hook in
    resolve-unbound-function-or-signal. Deferred: the undefined error
    already prevents silent BricsCAD-only behaviour.
  - Bricsys support ticket to confirm the setf-"2" sentinel is intentional
    and to pin `let's official support status.

== Progress (2026-08-09) — the last implementable item is DONE ==

The "TODO (low value)" item above — a dedicated
[ext-bricscad-undocumented] diagnostic for setf / let* — is implemented
(clautolisp 1.8.26), via the recognise-but-don't-define hook the note
predicted, now sitting in RESOLVE-UNBOUND-FUNCTION-OR-SIGNAL.

Both constructs remain intentionally UNDEFINED: calling them still
signals :undefined-function, unchanged, so nothing that matches on that
code is affected. What changed is that a note now precedes the error and
explains that the omission is deliberate:

  [ext-bricscad-undocumented] SETF is not implemented by clautolisp,
  deliberately: BricsCAD's SETF returns a constant sentinel 2 and does
  NOT assign; clautolisp declines to imitate it. Evidence:
  vendor:probes:bricscad:macos harvest against real BricsCAD V26,
  2026-08-07. ...

Design points worth keeping:

  - The note is NOT dialect-gated, unlike the advisory warnings. The
    error fires under every dialect, so the explanation is useful under
    every dialect — and a --dialect bricscad user is the MOST likely to
    be surprised, because on the real engine setf does exist. Tested
    across all five dialects.
  - No escalation knob: the outcome is already an error.
  - Deduped per label rather than per source occurrence, since an
    undefined-function error normally ends the run anyway.
  - Silent for any name the catalogue does not list, so ordinary
    undefined-function errors are untouched (regression test).

The knowledge behind it moved into the shared portability catalogue
(clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/portability.lisp) built for
clautolisp-dialect-portability-warnings — SETF and LET* are its two
:declined entries, carrying the harvest evidence string. LET, &REST and
bare-& are :provided entries there too, so all five harvest results now
live in one table rather than in scattered case forms.

REMAINING — external only, hence BLOCKED rather than open-with-work:

  - The Bricsys support ticket confirming that the setf-"2" sentinel is
    intentional and pinning `let''s official support status. That is a
    vendor conversation, not repository work; nothing in the tree is
    waiting on it, and the implemented behaviour does not depend on the
    answer (clautolisp declines setf either way).
